unicorn/no-nested-ternary

Disallow nested ternary expressions.

Why is this bad?
Nesting ternary expressions makes code harder to read: the reader has to hold every condition in mind to find which branch a value comes from. One level of nesting is allowed when it is wrapped in parentheses; anything deeper is reported.

Examples of incorrect code:
const foo = i > 5 ? i < 100 ? true : false : true;
const foo = i > 5 ? true : (i < 100 ? true : (i < 1000 ? true : false));

Examples of correct code:
const foo = i > 5 ? (i < 100 ? true : false) : true;

Docs: https://oxc.rs/docs/guide/usage/linter/rules/unicorn/no-nested-ternary.html
